Referral Criteria 
Severe cases of jaundice and those associated with abdominal pain 
must be referred for specialist care.
121.	Post-Partum Haemorrhage 
Post-partum haemorrhage may be primary or secondary. Primary 
postpartum haemorrhage refers to bleeding of more than 500 ml from the 
genital tract within the first twenty-four hours of delivery or any amount 
of blood loss that result in haemodynamic compromise of the patient. It 
usually occurs during or immediately after the third stage of labour.
Secondary post-partum haemorrhage is defined as excessive vaginal 
bleeding occurring from twenty-four hours to six weeks after delivery.
The bleeding may occur with the placenta retained or after its 
expulsion from the uterus. Postpartum haemorrhage becomes life 
threatening if the mother is already anaemic.  Blood loss of more than 
500 ml may lead to shock.
Causes 
yy
Uterine atony (70-90% of cases)
yy
Retention of all or part of placental tissue within the uterine cavity
yy
Infection within the uterine cavity (endo-myometritis)
yy
Genital tract trauma
yy
Clotting disorders
Symptoms
yy
Excessive or prolonged vaginal bleeding after delivery
yy
Lower abdominal pains 
Signs
yy
Active bleeding from the genital tract

— Post-Partum Haemorrhage  —
Chapter 13: Obstetric Care and Obstetric Disorders
345
yy
Conjunctival pallor
yy
Rapid pulse
yy
Blood pressure may be low or normal
yy
Deterioration of maternal levels of consciousness
yy
Flabby poorly contracted uterus
yy
Obvious tears in birth canal and/or perineum
yy
Obvious retained placenta
yy
Suprapubic tenderness 
Investigations
yy
FBC, sickling status 
yy
Bedside clotting test
yy
Blood grouping and cross-matching
yy
Ultrasound scan (if patient is stable to check for retained placenta 
tissue) 
Treatment 
Treatment objectives
yy
To identify the cause and stop bleeding as quickly as possible
yy
To correct hypotension
yy
To correct resulting anaemia
Non-pharmacological treatment
Due to uterine atony (70-90% of cases), with no placental retention 
yy
Massage fundus of uterus to stimulate contraction
yy
Encourage woman to empty bladder or pass a urethral catheter to 
empty the bladder and monitor urine output
yy
Bimanual compression of the uterus and balloon tamponade if 
uterus fails to contract with massage 
Due to retained placenta
yy
Attempt removal of the placenta by controlled cord traction as soon 
as a contraction is felt. If not successful await the next contraction 
and repeat the procedure
yy
If the placenta cannot be expelled in this fashion, manual removal 
under anaesthesia is indicated 
yy
If the placenta has been delivered and is incomplete, exploration the 
uterus and manual removal under anaesthesia is indicated
yy
If the facilities for manual removal of placenta under anaesthesia are 
not immediately available refer to hospital
Bleeding with uterus well contracted and placenta completely 
delivered 
yy
Examine the patient in the lithotomy position with adequate 
analgesia and/or anaesthesia, good lighting to identify and suture 
perineal, vaginal and cervical tears
yy
If the tear(s) extends into the uterine body, effective suturing cannot 

— Post-Partum Haemorrhage  —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
346
be performed and repair will involve a laparotomy
yy
For ruptured uterus, repair or hysterectomy is required
Bleeding associated with coagulopathy 
yy
Bedside clotting test - 5 ml of blood placed in a 10 ml round bottomed 
glass tube should clot in 6 minutes
Pharmacological treatment 
A.     If the uterus is poorly contracted (Atony)
1st Line Treatment
Evidence Rating: [A]
yy
Oxytocin, IM, 10 units stat. 
Then
yy
Oxytocin, IV, infusion, 10-40 units in 500 ml Dextrose saline or 0.9% 
or Normal saline
Note 13-6
Dose not to exceed 40 units
If intravenous oxytocin is unavailable, or if the bleeding does not respond to 
oxytocin, the following second line drugs are recommended.
2nd Line Treatment
Evidence Rating: [B]
yy
Misoprostol, sublingual, 600 microgram (for PPH prophylaxis within 
1 minute of delivery)
yy
Misoprostol, sublingual, 800 microgram stat if patient is conscious 
(for PPH treatment)
Or 
3rd Line Treatment
Evidence Rating: [B]
yy
Ergometrine, IV, 500 microgram stat.
Or
Evidence Rating: [B]
yy
Oxytocin-ergometrine, IM, (Oxytocin 10 units and Ergometrine 500 
microgram) stat.
Or
Evidence Rating: [B]
yy
Oxytocin-misoprostol, (Oxytocin, IV, 10 units and Misoprostol, rectal, 
600 micrograms) stat.
Note 13-7
High rates of adverse effects (nausea, vomiting, and high blood pressure) occur 
in women treated with ergometrine. 
They should not be given to women with hypertension in pregnancy or heart 
disease.

