('122','Post-Partum Pyrexia','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','349','349-351','yy Fever yy Other symptoms as related to cause','yy Fever yy Other signs as related to cause','yy FBC yy RDT yy Blood film for malaria parasites yy Blood for culture and sensitivity yy Urine for culture and sensitivity yy High vaginal swab yy Fasting or Random Blood Glucose yy Pelvic scan to exclude retained products of conception or pelvic abscess','Treatment objectives yy To identify and treat the underlying cause Non-pharmacological treatment yy Examination under anaesthesia with possible uterine curettage for retained products of conception yy Encourage frequent emptying of breasts if cause is due to engorgement yy Incision and drainage for breast and perianal abscess — Post-Partum Pyrexia — Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017 350 Pharmacological treatment A. Breast problems - engorgement Evidence Rating: [B] yy Paracetamol, oral, 1 g 6-8 hourly as required B. Breast problems - mastitis/abscess Evidence Rating: [A] yy Flucloxacillin, oral, 500 mg 6 hourly for 5-7 days C. Endo-myometritis and perineal infections Evidence Rating: [B] yy Amoxicillin + Clavulanic Acid, IV, 600 mg-1.2 g 8 hourly for 72 hours And yy Metronidazole, IV, 500 mg 8 hourly for 72 hours Then yy Amoxicillin + Clavulanic Acid, oral, 625 mg-1 g 12 hourly for 7 days And yy Metronidazole, oral, 400 mg 8 hourly for 7 days','Refer all cases of severe sepsis to hospital for management.','— Post-Partum Pyrexia  —
Chapter 13: Obstetric Care and Obstetric Disorders
349
122.	Post-Partum Pyrexia 
Post-partum pyrexia refers to body temperature of 38°C or more on 
2 or more occasions during the first 10 days of the puerperium excluding 
the first day. Risk Factors include prolonged labour, prolonged premature 
rupture of membranes, retained placental tissue, instrumental deliveries 
and birth canal injuries.
Causes 
yy
Malaria
yy
Uterine infection (endo-myometritis)
yy
Perineal Infections (e.g. infected episiotomy)
yy
Breast problems (engorgement, mastitis, abscess formation)
yy
Urinary tract infection
yy
Respiratory tract infection
Symptoms
yy
Fever
yy
Other symptoms as related to cause 
Signs
yy
Fever
yy
Other signs as related to cause
Investigations
yy
FBC
yy
RDT
yy
Blood film for malaria parasites
yy
Blood for culture and sensitivity 
yy
Urine for culture and sensitivity 
yy
High vaginal swab 
yy
Fasting or Random Blood Glucose
yy
Pelvic scan to exclude retained products of conception or pelvic 
abscess
Treatment 
Treatment objectives
yy
To identify and treat the underlying cause 
Non-pharmacological treatment
yy
Examination under anaesthesia with possible uterine curettage for 
retained products of conception
yy
Encourage frequent emptying of breasts if cause is due to 
engorgement
yy
Incision and drainage for breast and perianal abscess 

— Post-Partum Pyrexia  —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
350
Pharmacological treatment 
A.     Breast problems - engorgement
Evidence Rating: [B]
yy
Paracetamol, oral, 1 g 6-8 hourly as required
B.     Breast problems - mastitis/abscess
Evidence Rating: [A]
yy
Flucloxacillin, oral, 500 mg 6 hourly for 5-7 days
C.     Endo-myometritis and perineal infections 
Evidence Rating: [B]
yy
Amoxicillin + Clavulanic Acid, IV, 600 mg-1.2 g 8 hourly for 72 hours 
And
yy
Metronidazole, IV, 500 mg 8 hourly for 72 hours 
Then
yy
Amoxicillin + Clavulanic Acid, oral, 625 mg-1 g 12 hourly for 7 days
And
yy
Metronidazole, oral, 400 mg 8 hourly for 7 days 
Referral Criteria 
Refer all cases of severe sepsis to hospital for management.

— Post-Partum Pyrexia  —
Chapter 13: Obstetric Care and Obstetric Disorders
351
