Chapter 13: Obstetric Care and Obstetric Disorders
357
presentation, amniotic fluid volume (normal or reduced), and the 
placental site. Estimate the foetal weight.
yy
Urinalysis and culture 
Treatment 
Treatment objectives
yy
To prevent and/or treat infection
yy
To prevent labour if preterm and baby is very immature
yy
To improve foetal survival through improved foetal lung maturity 
Non-pharmacological treatment
yy
Bed rest
Pharmacological treatment 
A.	
Infection Prevention
1st Line Treatment
Evidence Rating: [B]
yy
Amoxicillin (Amoxycillin), oral, 500 mg 8 hourly for 7 days 
And 
yy
Metronidazole, oral, 400 mg 8 hourly for 7 days 
2nd Line Treatment
Evidence Rating: [C]
yy
Amoxicillin + Clavulanic Acid, oral, 625 mg - 1 g 12 hourly for 7 days 
B.	
Infection Prevention – patients with penicillin allergy
yy
Erythromycin, oral, 500 mg 6 hourly for 7 days
And 
yy
Metronidazole, oral, 400 mg 8 hourly for 7 days 
C.      Foetal lung maturation – for babies 28-34 weeks 
Evidence Rating: [A]
yy
Betamethasone, oral, 0.6-7 mg every 24 hours (2 doses)
Or 
yy
Dexamethasone, oral, 6 mg 12 hourly for (4 doses)  
Note 13-16
Treatment is most effective if delivery occurs at least 24 hours after the first 
dose of the medicine has been given and less than 7 days after the last dose of 
the medicine. They also benefit the premature newborn by lowering the risk of 
intraventricular hemorrhage and death. 
Referral Criteria 
If at sub-district level refer patients to hospital or specialist for 
further management if signs of maternal infection, pregnancy is less than 
37 weeks and or premature labour occurs.'),
('126','Dysmenorrhoea','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','358','358-358','yy Lower abdominal pain that is cramping or colicky in nature but may be dull and constant yy Pain may radiate to the lower back or legs yy Nausea, vomiting, headaches and dizziness may sometimes be associated with the pain','yy No typical physical signs','yy FBC yy Sickling yy Pelvic ultrasound scan to rule out pelvic lesions such as fibroids','Treatment objectives yy To relieve pain yy To treat underlying cause Non-pharmacological treatment yy Bed rest yy Warm pads applied to the lower abdomen',NULL,'14
Chapter
358
Gynaecological Disorders 
126.	Dysmenorrhoea 
Dysmenorrhea refers to cyclical lower abdominal pain associated 
with menstruation. The pain is thought to result from uterine contractions. 
It may be primary when there is no identifiable cause or secondary when 
associated with an underlying cause.
Causes 
yy
Often no underlying cause (primary) 
yy
Uterine fibroids
yy
Chronic pelvic infections e.g. Chlamydial infections
yy
Endometriosis
Symptoms
yy
Lower abdominal pain that is cramping or colicky in nature but may 
be dull and constant
yy
Pain may radiate to the lower back or legs
yy
Nausea, vomiting, headaches and dizziness may sometimes be 
associated with the pain
Signs
yy
No typical physical signs
Investigations
yy
FBC
yy
Sickling
yy
Pelvic ultrasound scan to rule out pelvic lesions such as fibroids 
Treatment 
Treatment objectives
yy
To relieve pain
yy
To treat underlying cause 
Non-pharmacological treatment
yy
Bed rest
yy
Warm pads applied to the lower abdomen'),
('127','Abortion','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','359','359-370','yy Scanty to moderate painless vaginal bleeding yy Mild pelvic discomfort','yy The uterine size is compatible with the gestational age — Abortion — Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017 360 yy There is no cervical effacement or dilatation','yy FBC and sickling yy Ultrasound scan (to confirm viable foetus in utero with closed cervix) yy Other investigations for potential underlying causes e.g. malaria','Treatment objectives yy To maintain a viable pregnancy to term if possible Non-pharmacological treatment yy Bed rest at home or hospital yy To abstain from sexual intercourse yy To report back if bleeding or pain increases Pharmacological treatment yy No specific treatment required yy Treat any underlying illnesses e.g. malaria INEVITABLE ABORTION Symptoms yy Lower abdominal pain yy Heavy vaginal bleeding yy No foetus or products of conception passed per vagina yy Painless loss of liquor per vaginam Signs yy The cervix is dilated with the membranes bulging yy There may be loss of liquor yy The uterine size is compatible with the gestational age yy There may be signs of shock pallor, collapsed peripheral vessels, rising pulse with reducing volume, falling BP and cold clammy skin Investigations yy FBC and sickling yy Blood grouping and cross matching yy Ultrasound scan (shows the foetus dead or alive) yy Cervix may be dilated with membranes bulging through it yy In instances associated with loss of liquor there may be oligohydraminios yy Ultrasound is necessary only if the diagnosis is in doubt Treatment Treatment objectives yy To resuscitate patient and/or prevent shock yy To relieve pain yy To allow the patient to abort (assist uterine contractions if weak) yy To evacuate the retained products of conception from the uterus — Abortion — Chapter 14: Gynaecological Disorders 361 yy To determine cause of abortion if recurrent yy To prevent infection with antibiotic prophylaxis yy To prevent risk of Rhesus incompatibility in future pregnancies Non-pharmacological treatment yy Evacuation of the uterus is done by either of the following techniques after the expulsion of the foetus or before the expulsion of the foetus if it is less than 12-14 weeks size yy Manual Vacuum Aspiration (MVA) with or without paracervical block anaesthesia Or yy Uterine curettage under paracervical block or general anaes­ thesia (Gestations 12 weeks or less yy Uterine evacuation under anaesthesia especially when the uterine size is larger than 12 weeks size Pharmacological treatment A. If patient is in shock or bleeding is severe yy IV fluids and blood transfusion as necessary B. To relieve severe pain Evidence Rating: [C] yy Morphine, IV, 2.5-5 mg 4 hourly as required And yy Metoclopramide, IV, 5-10 mg 8 hourly as required for vomiting Or yy Pethidine, IM, 75-100 mg stat. Then 50-100 mg 6-8 hourly if required And yy Promethazine, IV/IM, 25 mg as required (max. 25 mg 6 hourly) as required to reduce the chances of vomiting and to potentiate the analgesic effect of Pethidine C. Evacuate uterus If uterine size > 12-14 weeks Evidence Rating: [A] yy Oxytocin, IV, 10-20 units per litre of Normal saline Or Uterine size <12 weeks Evidence Rating: [C] yy Misoprostol, oral/SL, 600 microgram stat. D. To Prevent Infection yy Amoxicillin, oral, 500 mg 8 hourly for 5-7days And yy Metronidazole, oral, 400 mg 8 hourly for 5-7days — Abortion — Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017 362 E. To prevent Rhesus Isommunization in Rhesus negative women Evidence Rating: [A] yy Anti D Rh Immune Globulin, IM, 300 microgram (1,500 Units), stat. within 72 hours of abortion INCOMPLETE ABORTION Symptoms yy Passage of large blood clots and/or the foetus and some products per vaginam yy Severe lower abdominal pain Signs yy If bleeding is severe: Pallor and/or Shock (collapsed peripheral vessels, fast pulse, falling BP and cold clammy skin) yy Uterine size is smaller than the dates yy Cervix is dilated with the foetus already aborted yy Whole placenta or parts thereof may be present within the uterine cavity Investigations yy FBC and sickling yy Blood grouping and cross matching yy Ultrasound scan (to be requested if doubt exists in the diagnosis especially in early pregnancies) Treatment Treatment objectives yy To resuscitate patient yy To evacuate the retained products of conception from the uterus yy To prevent infection with antibiotic prophylaxis yy To determine cause of abortion, if recurrent yy To prevent risk of Rhesus incompatibility in future pregnancies Non-pharmacological treatment yy Digital curettage during vaginal examination to remove as much of the retained tissues as possible yy Surgical evacuation of retained products of conception e.g. manual vacuum aspiration (MVA) with or without anaesthesia yy Post abortion abstention from sexual intercourse for at least 2 weeks yy Post abortion counselling and psychological support (including Family Planning) Pharmacological treatment A. If in shock and/or severe bleeding yy IV fluids and blood transfusion as necessary B. Abortion with uterine size < 12 weeks Evidence Rating: [A] yy Ergometrine, IM/IV, 500 microgram stat. — Abortion — Chapter 14: Gynaecological Disorders 363 Or yy Misoprostol, oral, 600 microgram stat. Or yy Misoprostol, sublingual, 400 microgram stat. C. Abortion with uterine size > 12 weeks and ≤ 24 weeks Evidence Rating: [A] yy Misoprostol, oral, 600 micrograms stat. Or yy Misoprostol, sublingual, 400 micrograms stat. D. Abortion with uterine size > 24 weeks Evidence Rating: [B] yy Oxytocin, IV, 20 units into 1 L of Sodium Chloride 0.9% and infuse at 30-60 drops per minute Or yy Misoprostol, oral, 600 micrograms stat. Or yy Misoprostol, sublingual, 400 micrograms stat. E. To prevent infection yy Amoxicillin, oral, 500 mg 8 hourly for 5-7days And yy Metronidazole, oral, 400 mg 8 hourly for 5-7days F. To prevent infection – in patients with penicllin allergy yy Erythromycin, oral, 500 mg 8 hourly for 5-7days And yy Metronidazole, oral, 400 mg 8 hourly for 5-7days G. To prevent Rhesus Isommunization Evidence Rating: [A] (See section under ‘Inevitable Abortion’ above) COMPLETE ABORTION Symptoms yy Cessation or reduction of vaginal bleeding following heavy bleeding with passage of clots and/or the foetus and placenta. yy Resolution or abatement of pain Signs yy The uterus is smaller than the gestational age yy The cervix is closed and firm yy No pelvic tenderness Investigations yy FBC yy Blood grouping and cross matching yy Ultrasound scan: to confirm empty uterine cavity — Abortion — Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017 364 Treatment Treatment objectives yy To confirm abortion is complete yy To assess for and manage anaemia if present yy To assess for and manage pelvic infection if present yy To prevent risk of Rhesus incompatibility in future pregnancies Non-pharmacological treatment yy Counselling and psychological support (including family Planning) Pharmacological treatment yy Treat anaemia if present (See section on ‘Anaemia in Pregnancy’) yy Provide antibiotics if needed (See section on treatment of ‘Incomplete Abortion’) yy Provide Anti D Immune globulin if Indicated (See section under ‘Inev­ itable Abortion’ above) SEPTIC ABORTION This is a life threatening complication of abortion. Most often there is a history of criminal interference with the pregnancy. It may lead to complications such as septic shock, uterine damage, peritonitis, haemorrhage, disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), acute renal failure, adult respiratory distress syndrome, tetanus or gas gangrene. Causes yy Infected retained products of conception Symptoms yy Severe lower abdominal pain yy Fever yy Vomiting yy Headache yy Offensive, bloody vaginal discharge Signs yy Fever (but temperature may be normal) yy Tachycardia yy If in septic shock: low blood pressure yy Peritonism yy Bulky tender uterus yy Cervix may b