('131','Female Infertility','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','382','382-383','yy Inability to achieve pregnancy despite regular unprotected sex at least 2-3 times weekly yy Amenorrhoea (females) yy Impotence (males)','Female yy Absence of secondary sexual characteristics yy Virilisation changes (hirsutism, clitoromegaly, deepening of voice etc.) yy Galactorrhoea yy Abdominal masses due to uterine or ovarian enlargement yy Abnormal vaginal discharge indicative of infection Male yy Abnormal penile discharge indicative of infection yy Absence of secondary sexual characteristics yy Testicular abnormalities e.g. varicocoeles, small or absent testes — Female Infertility — Chapter 14: Gynaecological Disorders 383','yy FBC, Sickling yy High vaginal swab yy Blood glucose yy Hystero-Salpingogram (best done under fluoroscopic guidance). yy Semen analysis yy Serum progesterone level in mid-luteal phase (day 21-23 of menstrual cycle) to check for ovulation yy Thyroid function tests yy Further hormonal studies e.g. Serum LH, FSH, Serum Testosterone, Prolactin to be done by specialist','Treatment objectives yy To treat the underlying cause of the infertility if possible yy To achieve pregnancy within the shortest possible time Non-pharmacological treatment yy Counselling yy Uterine, Tubal and Testicular surgery where needed yy Assisted Reproduction Technologies e.g. Artificial insemination, In- vitro-fertilization with embryo transfer where indicated Pharmacological treatment A. Failure of ovulation 1st Line Treatment Evidence Rating: [A] yy Clomifene citrate, oral, 50 mg daily for 5 days, starting between the 2nd and 5th day of the menstrual cycle. B. Hyperprolactinaemia 1st Line Treatment Evidence Rating: [A] yy Bromocriptine, oral, 1.25 mg nocte for 7 days (with the evening meal or at bedtime). Increase weekly to a max. of 2.5 mg 8 hourly','Early referral of all patients with infertility to a specialist is preferred, particularly women with uterine, ovarian or tubal disease requiring surgery or who require ovulation induction not responsive to clomifene citrate. Also refer male partners for review by a urologist.','— Female Infertility —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
382
131.	Female Infertility
Primary infertility is said to occur when a couple has never achieved 
a pregnancy despite at least one year of uninterrupted and adequate 
unprotected sexual intercourse. Secondary infertility implies that there 
has been a previous pregnancy. It is often necessary to evaluate both 
partners simultaneously and to elicit sexual, menstrual and obstetric 
history, a past history of Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID) and Sexually 
Transmitted Infections (STIs), contraception use and other significant past 
medical history.
Causes 
Female factors
yy
Ovulation failure
yy
Polycystic ovarian syndrome (PCOS)
yy
Hyperprolactinaemia
yy
Pelvic factors
yy
Congenital malformations of uterus etc.
yy
Tubal disease
yy
Pelvic adhesions
yy
Uterine fibroids
yy
Endometriosis
yy
Cervical factors 
Male factors
yy
Oligospermia
yy
Azoospermia
yy
Penile and testicular abnormalities 
yy
Erectile dysfunction 
Symptoms
yy
Inability to achieve pregnancy despite regular unprotected sex at 
least 2-3 times weekly
yy
Amenorrhoea (females)
yy
Impotence (males)
Signs
Female
yy
Absence of secondary sexual characteristics 
yy
Virilisation changes (hirsutism, clitoromegaly, deepening of voice 
etc.) 
yy
Galactorrhoea
yy
Abdominal masses due to uterine or ovarian enlargement
yy
Abnormal vaginal discharge indicative of infection
Male
yy
Abnormal penile discharge indicative of infection
yy
Absence of secondary sexual characteristics 
yy
Testicular abnormalities e.g. varicocoeles, small or absent testes

— Female Infertility —
