yy
Further hormonal studies e.g. Serum LH, FSH, Serum Testosterone, 
Prolactin to be done by specialist
Treatment 
Treatment objectives
yy
To treat the underlying cause of the infertility if possible
yy
To achieve pregnancy within the shortest possible time
Non-pharmacological treatment
yy
Counselling
yy
Uterine, Tubal and Testicular surgery where needed
yy
Assisted Reproduction Technologies e.g. Artificial insemination, In-
vitro-fertilization with embryo transfer where indicated
Pharmacological treatment 
A.	
Failure of ovulation 
1st Line Treatment
Evidence Rating: [A]  
yy
Clomifene citrate, oral, 50 mg daily for 5 days, starting between the 
2nd and 5th day of the menstrual cycle.
B.	
Hyperprolactinaemia
1st Line Treatment
Evidence Rating: [A]
yy
Bromocriptine, oral, 1.25 mg nocte for 7 days (with the evening meal 
or at bedtime). Increase weekly to a max. of 2.5 mg 8 hourly 
Referral Criteria 
Early referral of all patients with infertility to a specialist is preferred, 
particularly women with uterine, ovarian or tubal disease requiring 
surgery or who require ovulation induction not responsive to clomifene 
citrate. Also refer male partners for review by a urologist.'),
('132','Menopause','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','384','384-385','yy Hot flushes (heat or burning in the face, neck and chest with resultant sweating) yy The flushes may be associated with yy Palpitations yy Faintness yy Dizziness yy Fatigue yy Weakness yy Emotional and psychological problems include: yy Mood changes yy Depression yy Anxiety yy Nervousness yy Irritability yy Loss of libido yy Vaginal dryness and dyspareunia yy Symptoms due to atrophic changes in the genital tract: yy Increased frequency of micturition and dysuria. yy Stress incontinence (urinary incontinence with coughing or straining)','yy Usually none — Menopause — Chapter 14: Gynaecological Disorders 385','yy Serum LH, FSH, Oestradiol yy Routine investigations e.g. FBC, blood glucose, lipid profile yy Urine or blood pregnancy tests (to exclude pregnancy)','Treatment objectives yy To control bothersome symptoms e.g. severe hot flushes, atrophic vaginitis and recurrent cystitis yy To prevent cardiovascular morbidity yy To prevent osteoporosis especially in individuals with premature menopause Non-pharmacological treatment yy Counselling and reassurance yy Encourage active lifestyles, healthy diet, exercise and regular physical checkups for common medical problems Pharmacological treatment A. Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT) for women with intact uterus 1st Line Treatment Evidence Rating: [A] yy Combined conjugated oestrogens and progestogen, oral, (28 tablets each containing conjugated oestrogens-625 micrograms including 12 tablets containing norgestrel-150 micrograms) One tablet daily Or yy Conjugated oestrogen and norgestrel tablet, oral, (625 microgram and 150 microgram) 1 tablet daily on days 17-28 of each 28-day treatment cycle; B. Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT) for women with previous hysterectomy Evidence Rating: [A] yy Conjugated oestrogens, oral, 625 microgram daily C. For relief of vaginal symptoms only Evidence Rating: [A] yy Oestrogen cream, vaginal, apply topically once daily Note 14-6 Women with intact uterus should never be given oestrogens alone. Current evidence suggests that hormone replacement therapy in the meno­ pause does not prevent coronary heart disease or strokes. HRT increases the risk of venous thrombo-embolic phenomena, breast cancer and endometrial cancer after prolonged use and should therefore be given for the shortest possible time whenever indicated',NULL,'— Menopause —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
384
132.	Menopause
Menopause refers to the point in time when permanent cessation 
of menstruation occurs due to loss of ovarian function. The cessation 
of menses is preceded by a peri-menopausal phase during which there 
may be menstrual irregularity. The age at onset is usually between 45 
and 55 years. A woman is considered to be menopausal if there has not 
been menstruation for a period of at least 12 months in the absence 
of pregnancy. Menopause may be associated with physical, emotional, 
and psychological upheaval of varying intensity in some women. Sixty 
percent of menopausal women however have mild symptoms or are 
asymptomatic. The risk of osteoporosis and cardiovascular disease 
increase after the menopause.
Causes 
yy
A natural life event due to the ageing of the individual
yy
Surgical removal of the ovaries (bilateral oophorectomy)
yy
Pelvic irradiation
yy
Premature ovarian failure 
yy
Pituitary damage from primary post-partum haemorrhage (Sheehan’s 
syndrome)
yy
Cytotoxic (anticancer) therapy
Symptoms
yy
Hot flushes (heat or burning in the face, neck and chest with resultant 
sweating) 
yy
The flushes may be associated with 
yy
Palpitations 
yy
Faintness
yy
Dizziness
yy
Fatigue
yy
Weakness
yy
Emotional and psychological problems include:
yy
Mood changes
yy
Depression
yy
Anxiety
yy
Nervousness
yy
Irritability
yy
Loss of libido
yy
Vaginal dryness and dyspareunia
yy
Symptoms due to atrophic changes in the genital tract: 
yy
Increased frequency of micturition and dysuria.
yy
Stress incontinence (urinary incontinence with coughing or 
straining)
Signs
yy
Usually none 

— Menopause —
Chapter 14: Gynaecological Disorders 
385
Investigations
yy
Serum LH, FSH, Oestradiol
yy
Routine investigations e.g. FBC, blood glucose, lipid profile
yy
Urine or blood pregnancy tests (to exclude pregnancy)
Treatment 
Treatment objectives
yy
To control bothersome symptoms e.g. severe hot flushes, atrophic 
vaginitis and recurrent cystitis
yy
To prevent cardiovascular morbidity
yy
To prevent osteoporosis especially in individuals with premature 
menopause
Non-pharmacological treatment
yy
Counselling and reassurance
yy
Encourage active lifestyles, healthy diet, exercise and regular physical 
checkups for common medical problems
Pharmacological treatment 
A.     Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT) for women with intact 
uterus 
1st Line Treatment 
Evidence Rating: [A]
yy
Combined conjugated oestrogens and progestogen, oral,
(28 tablets each containing conjugated oestrogens-625 micrograms 
including 12 tablets containing norgestrel-150 micrograms)
One tablet daily
Or 
yy
Conjugated oestrogen and norgestrel tablet, oral, 
(625 microgram and 150 microgram)
1 tablet daily on days 17-28 of each 28-day treatment cycle; 
B.     Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT) for women with previous 
hysterectomy 
Evidence Rating: [A]
yy
Conjugated oestrogens, oral, 625 microgram daily 
C.	
For relief of vaginal symptoms only
Evidence Rating: [A]
yy
Oestrogen cream, vaginal, apply topically once daily 
Note 14-6
Women with intact uterus should never be given oestrogens alone.  
Current evidence suggests that hormone replacement therapy in the meno­
pause does not prevent coronary heart disease or strokes.
HRT increases the risk of venous thrombo-embolic phenomena, breast cancer 
and endometrial cancer after prolonged use and should therefore be given for 
the shortest possible time whenever indicated'),
('133','Carcinoma of the Cervix','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','386','386-387','yy Asymptomatic (diagnosed on routine cervical screening) yy Symptomatic (advanced disease) yy Abnormal vaginal bleeding yy In between regular menstrual periods yy After sexual intercourse yy Post menopausal bleeding yy Abnormal vaginal discharge yy Lower abdominal pain yy Pain during sexual intercourse yy Weight loss yy Urinary symptoms e.g. dysuria, frequency, incontinence yy Rectal pain — Carcinoma of the Cervix — Chapter 14: Gynaecological Disorders 387','yy Early disease: Erosion of cervix or changes of chronic cervicitis yy Late/advanced disease: Ulcerative or fungating cervical lesion on speculum examination','yy Cervical biopsy yy FBC and sickling status yy Blood urea and electrolytes yy Serum Creatinine yy Serum uric acid yy Chest X-ray yy Ultrasound of Kidneys yy CT Scan and or Magnetic Resonance Imaging (to detect aortic nodes and metastases to the lungs and liver) yy Pelvic examination under anaesthesia yy Cystoscopy and proctoscopy with or without biopsy to allow visualisation of vesical or rectal mucosa','Treatment objectives yy To treat central tumour yy To treat areas of tumour spread with the aim of eradicating the disease Non-pharmacological treatment yy Surgery yy Radiotherapy yy A combination of surgery, and radiotherapy Pharmacological treatment Evidence Rating: [A] yy Adjuvant chemotherapy (See section on referral below)','Refer cases with osteoporosis or severe unremitting symptoms to the specialist. 133. Carcinoma of the Cervix Carcinoma of the cervix is the commonest form of female genital cancer seen in Ghana. While the disease is preventable, in Ghana, the absence of an effective screening system has resulted in most cases presenting late with advanced disease. In developed countries, the incidence of cervical cancer has fallen considerably due to regular screening procedures (e.g. Pap smear, visual inspection with acetic acid, Human Papilloma Virus [HPV] test). Vaccination against cancer-causing HPV infection is the ideal method for primary prevention of cervical cancer. Treatment of cervical cancer requires the services of a gynaecological oncologist and radiotherapist. Treatment includes surgery and/or radiation and/or chemotherapy. Surgery involves removal of the central tumour as well as the lymphatics draining the area (Wertheim’s hysterectomy). This extensive surgery should be carried out only by trained specialists. Radiation treatment often involves localized pelvic irradiation. Causes yy Human papilloma virus (accounts for 80% of cases) yy Associated risk factors include: yy Sexual promiscuity yy Multiple child births yy Infections with Herpes Simplex Hominis type II, HIV yy Smoking yy Low socio-economic status yy Family history Symptoms yy Asymptomatic (diagnosed on routine cervical screening) yy Symptomatic (advanced disease) yy Abnormal vaginal bleeding yy In between regular menstrual periods yy After sexual intercourse yy Post menopausal bleeding yy Abnormal vaginal discharge yy Lower abdominal pain yy Pain during sexual intercourse yy Weight loss yy Urinary symptoms e.g. dysuria, frequency, incontinence yy Rectal pain','— Carcinoma of the Cervix  —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
386
Referral Criteria 
Refer cases with osteoporosis or severe unremitting symptoms to 
the specialist.
133.	Carcinoma of the Cervix 
Carcinoma of the cervix is the commonest form of female genital 
cancer seen in Ghana. While the disease is preventable, in Ghana, the 
absence of an effective screening system has resulted in most cases 
presenting late with advanced disease. In developed countries, the 
incidence of cervical cancer has fallen considerably due to regular 
screening procedures (e.g. Pap smear, visual inspection with acetic acid, 
Human Papilloma Virus [HPV] test). 
Vaccination against cancer-causing HPV infection is the ideal method 
for primary prevention of cervical cancer. Treatment of cervical cancer 
requires the services of a gynaecological oncologist and radiotherapist. 
Treatment includes surgery and/or radiation and/or chemotherapy. 
Surgery involves removal of the central tumour as well as the lymphatics 
draining the area (Wertheim’s hysterectomy).  This extensive surgery 
should be carried out only by trained specialists. Radiation treatment 
often involves localized pelvic irradiation.
Causes 
yy
Human papilloma virus (accounts for 80% of cases)
yy
Associated risk factors include:
yy
Sexual promiscuity
yy
Multiple child births
yy
Infections with Herpes Simplex Hominis type II, HIV
yy
Smoking
yy
Low socio-economic status
yy
Family history
Symptoms
yy
Asymptomatic (diagnosed on routine cervical screening)
yy
Symptomatic  (advanced disease)
yy
Abnormal vaginal bleeding 
yy
In between regular menstrual periods
yy
After sexual intercourse
yy
Post menopausal bleeding
yy
Abnormal vaginal discharge
yy
Lower abdominal pain 
yy
Pain during sexual intercourse
yy
Weight loss
yy
Urinary symptoms e.g. dysuria, frequency, incontinence
yy
Rectal pain

— Carcinoma of the Cervix  —
Chapter 14: Gynaecological Disorders 
387
Signs
yy
Early disease:  Erosion of cervix or changes of chronic cervicitis
yy
Late/advanced disease: Ulcerative or fungating cervical lesion on 
speculum examination
Investigations
yy
Cervical biopsy
yy
FBC and sickling status
yy
Blood urea and electrolytes
yy
Serum Creatinine
yy
Serum uric acid
yy
Chest X-ray
yy
Ultrasound of Kidneys
yy
CT Scan and or Magnetic Resonance Imaging (to detect aortic nodes 
and metastases to the lungs and liver)
yy
Pelvic examination under anaesthesia
yy
Cystoscopy and proctoscopy with or without biopsy to allow 
visualisation of vesical or rectal mucosa
Treatment 
Treatment objectives
yy
To treat central tumour 
yy
To treat areas of tumour spread with the aim of eradicating the 
disease
Non-pharmacological treatment
yy
Surgery
yy
Radiotherapy 
yy
A combination of surgery, and radiotherapy 
Pharmacological treatment 
Evidence Rating: [A]
yy
Adjuvant chemotherapy (See section on referral below)
Referral Criteria 
All patients must be referred to a specialist for evaluation to decide 
on stage of disease and best mode of treatment. Treatment of carcinoma 
of the cervix is best done in hospital under specialist team care.'),
('134','Acute Glomerulonephritis','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','388','388-389','yy A history of preceding infection yy Generalized oedema most marked around the eyes yy Breathlessness yy Anorexia (sometimes associated with vomiting and abdominal pain) yy Fever yy Seizures yy Scanty urine yy Haematuria','yy Oedema yy Oliguria (urine volumes < 400 ml/day) yy Hypertension yy Haematuria yy Dark coloured urine yy Acute heart failure yy Coma','yy Urinalysis — Acute Glomerulonephritis — Chapter 14: Disorders of the Kidney and Genitourinary System 389 yy Sediment shows erythrocytes, leukocytes and a variety of casts including erythrocyte casts yy Proteinuria usually less than 2 g/24 hours but may be in the nephrotic range yy FBC yy BUE and Creatinine yy Throat cultures (in children may be useful) yy Chest X-ray (may show pulmonary oedema) yy ECG yy Immunology (e.g. ANA, AntiDsDNA) yy ASO (antistreptolysin O) titres yy Ultrasound of kidneys','Treatment objectives yy To identify and stop the cause of renal injury yy To prevent and control complications Non-pharmacological treatment yy Bed rest yy Salt restriction in diet yy Control fluid balance: Adults Control fluid retention by restricting daily fluid intake to 800 ml plus previous day’s urine output. Children Restrict fluids to 400 ml/m2 of body surface area and previous day’s urine output. Pharmacological treatment A. For fluid retention in Post-infectious Glomerulonephritis Evidence Rating: [B] yy Furosemide, oral or IV, Adult 40 mg daily, increasing to 80 mg daily Children 1 month-12 years; 1-2 mg/kg/day initially. Increase by 1-2 mg/kg 8 hourly to a max. of 6 mg/kg per day, not to exceed 80 mg per day. Neonates 0.5-1 mg/kg 8 to 24 hourly; max. 2 mg/kg. B. For hypertension in Post-infectious Glomerulonephritis yy Treat blood pressure (See section on ‘Hypertension’)','Refer all patients with complications of renal failure, severe cardiac failure and hypertensive encephalopathy that arise following post infectious glomerulonephritis to a physician specialist or a nephrologist.','14
Chapter
388
Disorders of the Kidney and 
Genitourinary System
134.	Acute Glomerulonephritis
This is a disease characterised by damage to the glomerular filtration 
apparatus, which causes protein and blood to leak into the urine. 
Mechanisms for the glomerular damage may be immune-mediated. This 
condition may be associated with hypertension and fluid retention.
Causes 
yy
Post streptococcal infections (pharyngeal or skin infections)
yy
Infected scabies
yy
Other bacterial Infections e.g. salmonella 
yy
Hepatitis B virus, Hepatitis C virus, HIV
yy
Parasitic e.g. Schistosoma, Malaria
yy
Systemic lupus erythematosus and other vasculitides
Symptoms
yy
A history of preceding infection
yy
Generalized oedema most marked around the eyes
yy
Breathlessness
yy
Anorexia (sometimes associated with vomiting and abdominal pain)
yy
Fever
yy
Seizures
yy
Scanty urine 
yy
Haematuria
Signs
yy
Oedema
yy
Oliguria (urine volumes < 400 ml/day)
yy
Hypertension
yy
Haematuria
yy
Dark coloured urine
yy
Acute heart failure
yy
Coma 
Investigations
yy
Urinalysis

— Acute Glomerulonephritis —
Chapter 14: Disorders of the Kidney and Genitourinary System
389
yy
Sediment shows erythrocytes, leukocytes and a variety of casts 
including erythrocyte casts
yy
Proteinuria usually less than 2 g/24 hours but may be in the nephrotic 
range
yy
FBC
yy
BUE and Creatinine
yy
Throat cultures (in children may be useful)
yy
Chest X-ray (may show pulmonary oedema)
yy
ECG
yy
Immunology (e.g. ANA, AntiDsDNA)
yy
ASO (antistreptolysin O) titres
yy
Ultrasound of kidneys
Treatment 
Treatment objectives
yy
To identify and stop the cause of renal injury
yy
To prevent and control complications
Non-pharmacological treatment
yy
Bed rest
yy
Salt restriction in diet
yy
Control fluid balance:
Adults
Control fluid retention by restricting daily fluid intake to 800 ml plus 
previous day’s urine output. 
Children
Restrict fluids to 400 ml/m2 of body surface area and previous day’s 
urine output.
Pharmacological treatment 
A. 	 For fluid retention in Post-infectious Glomerulonephritis 
Evidence Rating: [B]
yy
Furosemide, oral or IV, 
Adult
40 mg daily, increasing to 80 mg daily
Children
1 month-12 years; 1-2 mg/kg/day initially. Increase by 1-2 mg/kg 8 
hourly to a max. of 6 mg/kg per day, not to exceed 80 mg per day.
Neonates
0.5-1 mg/kg 8 to 24 hourly; max. 2 mg/kg.
B. 	 For hypertension in Post-infectious Glomerulonephritis
yy
Treat blood pressure (See section on ‘Hypertension’) 
Referral Criteria 
Refer all patients with complications of renal failure, severe cardiac 
failure and hypertensive encephalopathy that arise following post 
infectious glomerulonephritis to a physician specialist or a nephrologist.'),
('135','Nephrotic Syndrome','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','390','390-391','yy Early morning facial puffiness yy Generalized body swelling yy Foamy appearance of urine yy Weight gain (unintentional) yy Poor appetite','yy Periorbital, peripheral, genital oedema yy Ascites yy Pleural effusion yy Protein malnutrition particularly in children with long standing disease','yy Urinalysis yy BUE and creatinine yy Serum albumin yy Serum lipids yy Fasting blood glucose — Nephrotic Syndrome — Chapter 14: Disorders of the Kidney and Genitourinary System 391 yy Serology - Hepatitis B, C, HIV yy Hb electrophoresis yy Antinuclear antibody (ANA) yy Ultrasound of kidneys','Treatment objectives yy To relieve symptoms yy To treat underlying condition yy To prevent and manage complications yy To delay progressive kidney damage Non-pharmacological treatment yy Restrict salt intake yy Adequate protein diet; 0.6-0.8 g/kg body weight of 1st class protein (eggs, meat, fish, dairy products) per day for adult and 2-4 g/kg per day for children Pharmacological treatment A. For control of oedema 1st Line Treatment Evidence Rating: [C] yy Furosemide, IV, Adult 40-80 mg 8-12 hourly (max. 160 mg daily) Children Refer to specialist. Note 14-1 In children, furosemide is used in conjunction with albumin infusion only in ser­ vere oedema. Or yy Furosemide, oral, Adult 40 mg daily, increasing to 80 mg daily; max. 240 mg daily Children Refer to specialist. B. For control of resistant oedema yy Furosemide, IV or oral. (as above for adults and children) And yy Metolazone, oral, Adults 2.5-10 mg once daily Children Refer to specialist.',NULL,'— Nephrotic Syndrome —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
