('136','Acute Kidney Injury','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','392','392-395','yy Nausea and vomiting yy Oliguria yy Anuria yy Oedema yy Decreased appetite yy Metallic taste in mouth yy Hiccups yy Change in mood yy Flank pain yy Fatigue — Acute Kidney Injury — Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017 394 yy Diarrhoea','yy Usually no specific signs','yy Urinalysis yy FBC yy BUE and creatinine yy Serum uric acid yy Blood culture yy Urine culture yy Abdomino-pelvic ultrasound scan yy Plain X-ray of abdomen','Treatment objectives yy To recognise and correct reversible causes yy To prevent further renal injury yy To maintain a normal electrolyte and fluid volume milieu yy To remove toxic waste from the body (dialysis) Non-pharmacological treatment yy Nutrition: Give protein of high biological value at 40 g protein/day in adults. In children 0.8-1 g/kg of 1st class protein/day yy Daily weighing yy Maintain fluid balance yy Beware of hyperkalaemia - avoid potassium containing foods e.g. bananas, coconut Pharmacological treatment 1st Line Treatment Evidence Rating: [C] A. Treatment of hypovolaemia yy Sodium Chloride, 0.9%, IV, Or yy Ringers lactate (if there is no hyperkalaemia) in cases of diarrhoea and vomiting Or yy Blood transfusion (in severe bleeding) Or yy Plasma replacement (in cases of severe burns). Note 14-4 In children, give 10-20 ml/kg fluid boluses. B. To re-establish diuresis after adequate fluid replacement yy Furosemide, IV, — Acute Kidney Injury — Chapter 14: Disorders of the Kidney and Genitourinary System 395 Adult 40-80 mg stat. Children 2-4 mg/kg stat. Do not repeat. C. Control of hyperkalaemia Adult yy Calcium gluconate 10%, IV, 10-20 ml, slow IV, over 2-5 minutes And yy Soluble Insulin, IV, 10 units in 50-100 ml Dextrose 50% And yy Sodium bicarbonate, IV, 8.4% (50 mEq in 50ml) 1-2 ml/kg over 5 min­ utes Note 14-5 Do not mix calcium gluconate and bicarbonate in the same delivery system. Children 