143.	Bacterial Prostatitis
Prostatitis is inflammation of the prostate gland, which may be 
bacterial or abacterial.  Bacterial prostatitis is more common than 
abacterial prostatitis. It may present as an acute condition which may 
either be sexually transmitted or result from urethral reflux of infected 
urine into the prostatic ducts. Other potential sources may be bacteria 
spread from rectum or bloodstream.
If inadequately treated this may progress to chronic prostatitis. It is 
more common in men below below 50 years.

