Diclofenac, rectal, 100 mg 12 hourly'),
('149','Urethral Stricture','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','421','421-421','yy Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms (LUTS) e.g. poor urinary stream, split or splayed stream, reduced caliber of stream improved by straining, frequency and dysuria, post-void dribbling, incomplete emptying of bladder yy Urinary (overflow) incontinence yy Urinary retention (acute or chronic)','yy There may be none in uncomplicated cases yy Palpable induration of urethra yy Periurethral abscesses yy Extravasation of urine into scrotum and superficial tissue yy Urethrocutaneous fistulas/watering-can scrotum',NULL,NULL,'Refer to a urologist or surgical specialist for definitive treatment after initial management. 149. Urethral Stricture This refers to a narrowing or complete obstruction of the urethral lumen due to fibrosis (scarring). It is the second commonest cause of retention of urine in Ghana and the most common in young males usually resulting from previous inadequately treated STI. The commonest site is the anterior urethra i.e. bulbar and penile urethra in males. It may be complicated by periurethral abscess, superficial extravasation of urine and urethrocutaneous fistulae, retention of urine and post-renal renal failure. Causes yy Gonococcal or non-gonococcal urethritis yy External trauma e.g. road traffic injuries, falls, straddle or astride injury and pelvic fractures yy Iatrogenic: Urethral instrumentation e.g. catheterisation, endoscopy yy Postoperative e.g. prostatectomy yy Congenital strictures (rare) Symptoms yy Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms (LUTS) e.g. poor urinary stream, split or splayed stream, reduced caliber of stream improved by straining, frequency and dysuria, post-void dribbling, incomplete emptying of bladder yy Urinary (overflow) incontinence yy Urinary retention (acute or chronic) Signs yy There may be none in uncomplicated cases yy Palpable induration of urethra yy Periurethral abscesses yy Extravasation of urine into scrotum and superficial tissue yy Urethrocutaneous fistulas/watering-can scrotum','— Urethral Stricture —
Chapter 14: Disorders of the Kidney and Genitourinary System
421
And
yy
Hyoscine butylbromide, IV, 20 mg 8 hourly 
Or 
yy
Mebeverine, oral, 135 mg 8 hourly may be useful
Caution 14-1.
Avoid morphine as it may cause further ureteric spasm and worsening of symp­
toms
yy
Give antibiotics if urinary tract infection is present. (See section on 
‘Urinary Tract Infection’)
Referral Criteria 
Refer to a urologist or surgical specialist for definitive treatment after 
initial management.
149.	Urethral Stricture
This refers to a narrowing or complete obstruction of the urethral 
lumen due to fibrosis (scarring). It is the second commonest cause of 
retention of urine in Ghana and the most common in young males usually 
resulting from previous inadequately treated STI. The commonest site is 
the anterior urethra i.e. bulbar and penile urethra in males. It may be 
complicated by periurethral abscess, superficial extravasation of urine 
and urethrocutaneous fistulae, retention of urine and post-renal renal 
failure.
Causes
yy
Gonococcal or non-gonococcal urethritis
yy
External trauma e.g. road traffic injuries, falls, straddle or astride 
injury and pelvic fractures
yy
Iatrogenic: Urethral instrumentation e.g. catheterisation, endoscopy
yy
Postoperative e.g. prostatectomy
yy
Congenital strictures (rare)
Symptoms
yy
Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms (LUTS) e.g. poor urinary stream, split 
or splayed stream, reduced caliber of stream improved by straining, 
frequency and dysuria, post-void dribbling, incomplete emptying of 
bladder
yy
Urinary (overflow) incontinence
yy
Urinary retention (acute or chronic)
Signs
yy
There may be none in uncomplicated cases
yy
Palpable induration of urethra 
yy
Periurethral abscesses
yy
Extravasation of urine into scrotum and superficial tissue
yy
Urethrocutaneous fistulas/watering-can scrotum'),
('150','Vasectomy (Male Sterilisation)','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','422','422-422',NULL,NULL,'yy Urinalysis yy Urine culture and sensitivity yy Blood urea, electrolytes and creatinine yy Ultrasound Scan of kidneys, bladder, postvoid residual urine and spongiofibrosis yy Retrograde urethrogram yy Antegrade urethrogram/Micturating Cysto-uretrogram (MCUG) provided a suprapubic catheter is in place yy Uroflowmetry yy Urethrocystoscopy','Treatment objectives yy To relieve symptoms and prevent complication yy To treat underlying cause Non-pharmacological treatment yy Try catheterisation - a gentle attempt is made to pass a urethral catheter, which will be held up, at the site of stricture in patients who present with retention of urine. Confirmation of site of obstruction is still needed by urethrography or urethroscopy. yy If catherization fails and patient is in acute retention yy Suprapubic cystostomy or suprapubic needle puncture and aspiration (try this procedure if facilities for suprapubic cystostomy are lacking). Aspirate as much urine as possible to decompress the bladder and relieve pain before referral yy Definitive treatment is surgical. In most cases referral to a specialist centre will be necessary Pharmacological treatment yy None except for Urinary Tract Infection (See appropriate section)','Refer to specialist for further investigations prior to definitive treatment. 150. Vasectomy (Male Sterilisation) Vasectomy is a permanent male contraceptive method, which is a simple, short and safe surgical procedure. It is carried out by trained surgeons usually under local anaesthesia after careful counselling and informed consent.','— Vasectomy (Male Sterilisation)  —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
422
yy
Bladder may be palpable if there is retention
yy
Kidney may be palpable in hydronephrosess
yy
Localized induration may be felt along the urethra
yy
Failure of catheterisation - this heightens the suspicion of a stricture
Investigations
yy
Urinalysis
yy
Urine culture and sensitivity
yy
Blood urea, electrolytes and creatinine
yy
Ultrasound Scan of kidneys, bladder, postvoid residual urine and 
spongiofibrosis
yy
Retrograde urethrogram 
yy
Antegrade urethrogram/Micturating Cysto-uretrogram (MCUG) 
provided a suprapubic catheter is in place
yy
Uroflowmetry
yy
Urethrocystoscopy
Treatment
Treatment objectives
yy
To relieve symptoms and prevent complication
yy
To treat underlying cause
Non-pharmacological treatment
yy
Try catheterisation -  a gentle attempt is made to pass a urethral 
catheter, which will be held up, at the site of stricture in patients who 
present with retention of urine. Confirmation of site of obstruction is 
still needed by urethrography or urethroscopy.
yy
If catherization fails and patient is in acute retention
yy
Suprapubic cystostomy or suprapubic needle puncture and aspiration 
(try this procedure if facilities for suprapubic cystostomy are lacking). 
Aspirate as much urine as possible to decompress the bladder and 
relieve pain before referral
yy
Definitive treatment is surgical. In most cases referral to a specialist 
centre will be necessary
Pharmacological treatment 
yy
None except for Urinary Tract Infection (See appropriate section)
Referral Criteria 
Refer to specialist for further investigations prior to definitive 
treatment.
150.	Vasectomy (Male Sterilisation) 
Vasectomy is a permanent male contraceptive method, which is 
a simple, short and safe surgical procedure. It is carried out by trained 
surgeons usually under local anaesthesia after careful counselling and 
informed consent.'),
('151','Acute Epididymo-orchitis','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','423','423-424','yy Fever yy Scrotal/testicular pain yy Scrotal swelling yy Urethral discharge yy Dysuria yy Malaise','yy Fever yy Tender and swollen hemiscrotum yy Inflamed epididymis and testis yy Secondary hydrocoele yy Positive Prehn’s sign (lifting of scrotum towards pubic symphysis in the palm relieves pain)','yy Urinalysis yy Urine culture and sensitivity - first catch of urine preferred to midstream urine yy FBC and ESR yy Blood culture and sensitivity yy Scrotal ultrasound/MRI','Treatment objectives yy To relieve symptoms yy To eradicate the infection yy To prevent recurrence yy To prevent complications e.g. abscess and sterility Non-pharmacological treatment yy Bed rest','Clients should be referred to a Family Planning Unit or Urologist for the procedure. 151. Acute Epididymo-orchitis This is an acute inflammation of the epididymis and testis usually due to a bacterial infection. It may follow ascending infection from the urethra (including STIs), instrumentation/catheterization, untreated lower urinary','— Acute Epididymo-orchitis —
Chapter 14: Disorders of the Kidney and Genitourinary System
423
Vasectomy is the most effective male family planning method. 
Involving males in issues of reproductive health and family planning has 
several benefits with a positive impact on society. 
Vasectomy should be encouraged for appropriate clients. It is less 
invasive and simpler than female sterilisation. Reversal of vasectomy is a 
difficult surgery using loupes/microscope with success rate of 60-80% if 
done in less than 3 years after vasectomy.
Box 14-1: 
Misconceptions
yy
Vasectomy is ligation of the vas deferens and NOT CASTRATION
yy
Vasectomy does not affect erection
yy
Vasectomy does not affect ejaculation and orgasm. There would be normal 
ejaculation but the semen does not contain spermatozoa 
yy
Vasectomy does not work immediately. A back-up method of contraception 
is necessary for up to 20 ejaculations, 3 months after the procedure or until 
examination of semen shows no sperm
yy
After vasectomy males will still require the use of condoms to prevent sexu­
ally transmitted infections including HIV-AIDS
Box 14-2: 
Effectiveness rates of various male contraceptive methods:
Evidence Rating: [B]
yy
Vasectomy - 99.85%
yy
Male condom - 86%
yy
Withdrawal method - 81% 
Preoperative requirements
yy
Detailed counselling (ideally both partners must be present) and informed 
consent
yy
Counselling should mention irreversibility of procedure, possible recanaliza­
tion and risk of chronic scrotal pain postoperative.
yy
Medical history
yy
Physical examination
yy
Laboratory investigations e.g. Hb, sickling, urinalysis
yy
Histopathology confirmation of the removed segment of vas deferens may 
be necessary for medicolegal reasons.
Referral Criteria 
Clients should be referred to a Family Planning Unit or Urologist for 
the procedure.
151.	Acute Epididymo-orchitis
This is an acute inflammation of the epididymis and testis usually due 
to a bacterial infection. It may follow ascending infection from the urethra 
(including STIs), instrumentation/catheterization, untreated lower urinary 

— Acute Epididymo-orchitis —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
424
tract obstruction and genito-urinary surgery. It is a known complication of 
mumps. Poorly managed acute epididymo-orchitis may be complicated 
by septicaemia, abscess formation, chronic epididymo-orchitis, secondary 
hydrocoele, infertility and Fournier’s gangrene. 
Before managing as acute epidymoorchitis make sure testicular 
torsion has been conclusively excluded.
