('154','Bladder Cancer','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','429','429-430','yy May be asymptomatic in early disease (25%) yy Haematuria (usually painless). Painful only in advanced disease and infection/UTI yy Irritative symptoms: frequency, urgency, dysuria yy Flank pain (hydronephrosis) yy Pelvic pain from cancer invasion yy Oedema of lower limbs from advanced disease','yy Pallor yy Wasting yy Palpable bladder mass yy Palpable kidney from ureteric obstruction and hydronephrosis yy Lymphoedema of lower limb/limbs yy Secondary UTI in 30% of cases','yy FBC and ESR yy Urine analysis and culture yy Urine cytology yy Urea, creatinine, electrolytes yy Ultrasound scan: Abdominopelvic yy CT Scan /MRI for staging (By specialist) yy Special Investigations: Abdominopelvic IVU yy Urethrocystoscopy and biopsies (By specialist) yy Examination under anaesthesia (Bimanual palpation of bladder through DRE and Pelvic examinations).','Treatment objectives yy Surgical cure for early disease yy Prevention of recurrence, progression and metastases yy Management of complications','Refer all cases with septic shock after resuscitation and all those who require reconstructive surgery to a urologist or surgical specialist. 154. Bladder Cancer Bladder cancer is the second commonest urological cancer after prostate cancer. It is the commonest of all the cancers, which affect the urinary tract lining (urothelium). Males are more affected than females in a ratio of about 3:1. It is more common in the white race compared to the black race in a ratio of 4:1. More than 80% of clients with bladder cancer are above 50 years. The commonest pathological types are','— Bladder Cancer —
