yy
Urethrocystoscopy and biopsies (By specialist)
yy
Examination under anaesthesia (Bimanual palpation of bladder 
through DRE and Pelvic examinations).
Treatment
Treatment objectives
yy
Surgical cure for early disease
yy
Prevention of recurrence, progression and metastases
yy
Management of complications'),
('155','Carcinoma of Prostate','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','431','431-432','yy Asymptomatic: prostate cancer may be present without symptoms in the early stage yy Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms (LUTS) and IPSS — Carcinoma of Prostate — Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017 432 yy Retention of urine yy Haematuria yy General debility anorexia, weight loss, listlessness yy Bone pain (commonly in the waist or limbs) yy Paralysis in the lower limbs or inability to walk yy Pathological Fracture yy Impotence yy Haemospermia yy Tenesmus','On DRE clinical signs include; yy Hard prostate gland with an irregular surface and edges yy Obliterated median sulcus yy Adherent rectal mucosa Advanced or metastatic disease: yy Anaemia yy Uraemia yy Wasting yy Bone tenderness yy Paraplegia yy Pathological fracture','yy FBC yy Blood urea, electrolytes and creatinine yy Prostate Specific Antigen (PSA) yy Liver function tests yy Abdominal and pelvic ultrasound yy Transrectal Ultrasound (TRUS) of the prostate, if available yy Transrectal needle biopsy of the prostate yy TRUS-guided or finger-guided','Treatment objectives yy To relieve symptoms yy To control complications yy To achieve cure for early disease yy To prevent local progression and metastases Non-pharmacological treatment yy Urethral catheterisation to relieve urinary retention where needed yy Radical prostatectomy or radiotherapy, under specialist care, for early disease yy Surgical castration (bilateral orchidectomy) for advanced disease Pharmacological treatment Evidence Rating: [A] yy Pharmacological treatment of carcinoma of the prostate, which','Refer all cases of bladder cancer for specialist evaluation and treatment. All cases of chronic cystitis should be referred to specialist to exclude bladder cancer. 155. Carcinoma of Prostate Ninety-five per cent of these tumours are adenocarcinomas. The majority of men affected are aged between 65 and 85 years. The incidence increases with age. It is recommended that every male, 40 years and above, should have annual screening by Prostate Specific Antigen (PSA) tests and Digital Rectal Examination (DRE) since early detection is associated with better prognosis. The benefit or otherwise of screening should be discussed with patients. Patients with a family history of prostate cancer and should consider annual screening from 40 years and above with PSA and DRE. It is worth noting that not every hard prostate on DRE is malignant. Likewise a normal-feeling prostate does not exclude a malignancy. A prostatic biopsy is therefore necessary to establish a diagnosis. Causes yy Ageing yy Functional testes yy Family history of prostate cancer, breast cancer, ovarian cancer yy Race (more common in blacks) yy High dietary fat intake Symptoms yy Asymptomatic: prostate cancer may be present without symptoms in the early stage yy Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms (LUTS) and IPSS','— Carcinoma of Prostate —
Chapter 14: Disorders of the Kidney and Genitourinary System
431
yy
Additional treatment with cancer drugs and radiotherapy where 
necessary (Neoadjuvant and adjuvant therapy)
