Goserelin, SC, preferably in the abdominal wall (refer to specialist)
Or
yy
Leuprolide acetate, IM, (refer to specialist)
And 
yy
Biphosphonates and Fentanyl patches (refer to specialist)
Referral Criteria 
Refer all cases to a specialist centre for evaluation and management.
156.	Erectile Dysfunction
It means the persistent inability of a man to achieve an erection, 
which is adequate in terms of hardness and duration for satisfactory 
sexual intercourse.  So long as a man can achieve a hard enough erection 
to permit vaginal penetration, with a long enough “staying power” 
to perform the sexual act till ejaculation is attained, he is judged to be 
potent. The number of “rounds” per session is irrelevant.
The condition may be classified as organic, psychogenic or mixed in 
terms of aetiology; primary (never been able to attain and/or maintain 
an erection for satisfactory sexual intercourse) or secondary, where 
impotence occurs in men who have previously had a satisfactory sexual 
performance.
Causes 
Psychogenic
yy
Anxiety
yy
Depression
yy
Stress
yy
Marital conflict
Organic
yy
Vasculogenic: arterial insufficiency/occlusion; venous incompetence
yy
Neurogenic: peripheral neuropathy; spinal cord lesions
yy
Traumatic: penectomy; pelvic fracture (with urethral rupture); 

— Erectile Dysfunction —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
434
perineal trauma
yy
Endocrine: diabetes mellitus, hypogonadism; hyperprolactinaemia; 
pituitary, adrenal and thyroid disorders
yy
Drugs: e.g. antihypertensives, antidepressants
yy
Post-operative: 
Cystectomy, 
Radical 
Prostatectomy; 
Abdominoperineal resection
yy
Inflammation; urethritis; prostatitis
yy
Mechanical: congenital penile abnormalities; Peyronies disease
yy
Endurance related: heart/renal/liver failure; pulmonary insufficiency 
yy
Post-priapism 
Symptoms
yy
Inability to achieve erection
yy
Inability to sustain erection
yy
Reduced sexual desire
Signs
yy
