159.	Urinary Schistosomiasis
This is a water-borne disease caused by penetration of the skin 
or mucous membranes by the early stages of the causative organism 
(Schistosoma haematobium), which in the adult form settles in the blood 
vessels of the urinary bladder resulting in the common presentation of 
haematuria.  
This disease is common in Ghana with several endemic areas along 
the lakes, slow-flowing rivers and irrigation systems.  The commonest 
body sites affected are the bladder, ureters and pelvic organs. Prevention 
entails avoiding contact with infested water.
Chronic infestation may lead to severe anaemia, ureteric stricture 
and hydronephrosis as well as carcinoma of the bladder.
Cause
yy
Schistosoma haematobium
Symptoms
Initial
yy
Itching and redness of skin at site of penetration of parasite
yy
Fatigue, low grade fever, malaise, lassitude, excessive sweating, 
headache and backache
Later
yy
Terminal haematuria
yy
Painful urination (dysuria)
yy
Lower abdominal pain (bladder pain)
Signs
yy
Pallor
yy
Palpable kidney from hydronephrosis due to ureteric stricture
yy
Palpable bladder from bladder cancer or retention of urine due to 
clots or bladder neck stenosis
yy
DRE may reveal a fibrosed prostate, enlarged seminal vesicle or 
thickened bladder base
Investigations
yy
FBC
yy
Urine for red blood cells, pus cells, and schistosoma ova (mid-day 
urine specimen preferably taken after physical exercise is ideal)
yy
Midstream urine for culture in associated urinary tract infections
yy
Imaging: Ultrasound scan; Intravenous Urogram (IVU) may show'),
