('161','Retention of Urine','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','440','440-441','yy Retention of urine in BPH precipitated by postponement of micturition, alcohol abuse and infection yy Sudden inability to pass urine with painful bulge in lower abdomen for acute retention of urine yy Gradual onset of inability to pass urine with painless bulge in lower abdomen for chronic retention of urine yy History of urethritis, urethral instrumentation (e.g. catheterisation) or perineal injury, pelvic fracture or surgery in urethral strictures yy International Prostate Symptom Score (IPSS)','yy Palpable tender bladder in acute retention of urine yy Palpable non-tender bladder for chronic retention of urine yy Uraemia/Azotaemia in renal failure yy Fever in infections: UTI and prostatitis yy Palpable kidneys in hydronephrosis yy Haematuria in BPH, prostate cancer and urethral strictures',NULL,'Treatment objectives yy To eliminate the causative organism yy To manage the complications Non-pharmacological treatment yy Avoid repeated exposure to infested water bodies if possible Pharmacological treatment Evidence Rating: [A] yy Praziquantel, oral, Adults and Children 40 mg/kg as a single dose yy Treat anaemia if present (See section on ‘Anaemia’)','Refer patient after adequate treatment if: haematuria and/ or symptoms of urinary infection persist or if complications like hydronephrosis, bladder mass, retention of urine, severe wasting and severe anaemia are present. 160. Persistent or Recurrent Urethral Discharge (See section on ‘STI-related persistent or recurrent urethral discharge’). 161. Retention of Urine Retention of urine is inability to empty a full bladder. It is the commonest urological emergency in Ghana and worldwide. Adult males are more commonly affected. It is rare in children and in females. The causes differ in different age groups as well as in males and females. A family history of retention of urine in Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH), prostate cancer, breast cancer and ovarian cancer are risk factors. Retention of urine can be classified into three: 1. Acute retention of Urine is of sudden onset with a palpable tender bladder. 2. Chronic Retention of Urine is of insidious onset and presents with a palpable non-tender bladder and overflow incontinence resulting in more damage to the bladder, ureters and kidneys. Post-renal renal failure may complicate this type of retention with hydronephroses and hydroureters. 3. Acute-on-chronic retention of urine is when a chronic retention of urine is suddenly complicated by acute retention of urine.','— Persistent or Recurrent Urethral Discharge  —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
